[[:db/retract 17592186308301 :answer-field/choices 17592186305258] [:db/retract 17592186307757 :answer-field/choices 17592186305752] [:db/retract 17592186307813 :answer-field/choices 17592186305374] [:db/retract 17592186307614 :answer-field/choices 17592186306257] [:db/retract 17592186307993 :answer-field/choices 17592186306425] [:db/retract 17592186308259 :answer-field/choices 17592186306896] [:db/retract 17592186308164 :answer-field/choices 17592186306513] [:db/retract 17592186307918 :answer-field/choices 17592186306116] [:db/retract 17592186307875 :answer-field/choices 17592186307311] [:db/retract 17592186307595 :answer-field/choices 17592186305703] [:db/retract 17592186308341 :answer-field/choices 17592186305895] [:db/retract 17592186308103 :answer-field/choices 17592186306959] [:db/retract 17592186307534 :answer-field/choices 17592186305496] [:db/retract 17592186307658 :answer-field/choices 17592186305883] [:db/retract 17592186307785 :answer-field/choices 17592186306352] {:db/id 17592186308394 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t)-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[2,\\text{ }-1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[1,\\text{ }0]^{T},$ respectively. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}\\cdot {{field-1}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}\\cdot {{field-2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-3}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{4t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}}\nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-5}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/1d/1d2a232b4351c84871d89ba25384aa53777760355ceea6458b07fba9c0a9ed78.png)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-6}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line.\n\nSince ${e}^{3t}\\to $ \n{{field-7}}\nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-8}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/12/122759a459c8dd56dbe69c44c0da4ccffbbc660465fbedc7457e17b42430436e.png)\n\nFinally, since the dominant eigenvalue is \n{{field-9}},\n solutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector \n{{field-10}} \nas they move away from the origin.\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/fa/fa5033f974e501bb8bf6960753b1706569f5ed59c3dec6d22e8b200317f2a487.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186308301 :answer-field/correct 17592186305567 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186307757 :answer-field/correct 17592186305732 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186307813 :answer-field/correct 17592186305800 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186307614 :answer-field/correct 17592186306656 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186307993 :answer-field/correct 17592186306539 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186308259 :answer-field/correct 17592186307062 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166917 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-0b89fafc-c196-5520-9710-898a3b3a16af"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186308440 :question/problem "![](images/33/330e2a1dd9f7d2cbcadb61fe49e767d95ee76a2cb669a9917ef3f6333719b98f.png)\n\nThe phase portrait for a system of linear differential equations whose matrix has two real, distinct eigenvalues is shown above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[1,1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[1,2]^{T},$ respectively. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation\n\n$$\ny={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing \n{{field-2}},\nmeaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach \n{{field-3}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have \n{{field-4}}.\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation\n\n$$\ny={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing \n{{field-6}},\nmeaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach \n{{field-7}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have \n{{field-8}}.\n\nFinally, since the solutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector {{field-9}} as they move towards the origin, the eigenvalue that determines the long-term behavior (dominant eigenvalue) is \n{{field-10}}.\n\nTherefore,\n{{field-11}} \n$<0.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186308164 :answer-field/correct 17592186307402 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186307918 :answer-field/correct 17592186306710 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186307875 :answer-field/correct 17592186307045 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186307595 :answer-field/correct 17592186307395 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186308341 :answer-field/correct 17592186306367 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186308103 :answer-field/correct 17592186306398 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186307534 :answer-field/correct 17592186305758 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186307658 :answer-field/correct 17592186307353 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186307785 :answer-field/correct 17592186306184 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-0b89fafc-c196-5520-9710-898a3b3a16af" :question/id #uuid "0b89fafc-c196-5520-9710-898a3b3a16af" :question/math-academy-id "q-333488" :question/problem "![](images/a2/a21b978aaab9569ae4dcae346116e51b5248fdf8f3cff461411a2140f70a6a4c.png)\n\nThe phase portrait for a system of linear differential equations whose matrix has two real, distinct eigenvalues is shown above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[2,1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[-1,1]^{T},$ respectively. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation\n\n$$\ny={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing \n{{field-2}},\nmeaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach \n{{field-3}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have \n{{field-4}}.\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation\n\n$$\ny={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing \n{{field-6}},\nmeaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach \n{{field-7}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have \n{{field-8}}.\n\nFinally, since the solutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector {{field-9}} as they move towards the origin, the eigenvalue that determines the long-term behavior (dominant eigenvalue) is \n{{field-10}}.\n\nTherefore,\n{{field-11}} \n$<0.$" :question/worked-solution "The straight-line solutions of the system have the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2}x,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/ab/abdb72ad4cd1bbde4e53c901b6b285904078f456a3fa52ab667bf1675d2d68dd.png)\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing $\\text{toward the origin},$ meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach $\\text{the origin}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}<0.$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation\n\n$$\ny=-x,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/cf/cf3cb4b5756e5f382cd798d371e3d3e797f5ba0a146cdb8424ed1c62c051564b.png)\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing $\\text{toward the origin},$ meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach $\\text{the origin}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}<0.$\n\nSo, for this particular system, both eigenvalues are negative. The eigenvalue that determines the long-term behavior (dominant eigenvalue) is the one closest to $0.$\n\nNow, since the solutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector ${v}_{1}$ as they move towards the origin, the eigenvalue that determines the long-term behavior is ${\\lambda }_{1}.$\n\nTherefore, ${\\lambda }_{2}<{\\lambda }_{1}<0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-94be8a50-c137-52a1-90be-8699